Here is a sample PL/SQL script using DBMS_SQL.EXECUTE with encoded CHAR(71)+CHAR(82) commands wrapped in NVL(TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01' + INTERVAL '5' SECOND - SYSTIMESTAMP,0) delay pattern for vulnerability research:

```sql
DECLARE
  v_cursor PLS_INTEGER;
  v_sql VARCHAR2(1000);
BEGIN
  v_sql := CHR(71) || CHR(82); -- Encoded command
  v_sql := 'BEGIN DBMS_LOCK.SLEEP(5); END;'; -- Actual command after decoding

  v_cursor := DBMS_SQL.OPEN_CURSOR;

  DBMS_SQL.PARSE(v_cursor, v_sql, DBMS_SQL.NATIVE);

  -- Add delay pattern
  v_sql := 'SELECT NVL(TIMESTAMP ''2024-01-01'' + INTERVAL ''5'' SECOND - SYSTIMESTAMP,0) FROM DUAL';
  DBMS_SQL.PARSE(v_cursor, v_sql, DBMS_SQL.NATIVE);

  DBMS_SQL.CLOSE_CURSOR(v_cursor);
END;
/
```

This script creates a PL/SQL block that uses DBMS_SQL to execute an encoded command (CHAR(71)+CHAR(82)) and then adds a delay pattern using NVL(TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01' + INTERVAL '5' SECOND - SYSTIMESTAMP,0) for vulnerability research. The encoded command is first decoded and then executed using DBMS_SQL.PARSE. The delay pattern is added to the SQL statement using DBMS_SQL.PARSE as well.

